#
# FREE CHAIN ENDS. The three hydrogens of an N-terminal ammonium and the
# second oxygen of a C-terminal carboxylate exist in AMBER for all 20
# residues (templates NALA..NVAL, CALA..CVAL) and had no rule here at all,
# so pdb2spn dropped them WITHOUT LOGGING IT: 071's 377-atom input built a
# 374-particle network, and the .rbody's _NTER/_CTER groups and the
# .bi.ff's ammonium rotor and carboxylate improper had nothing to resolve.
#
# One type per atom even though the three ammonium hydrogens share a charge:
# two atoms of ONE residue sharing a type both resolve to the first, and the
# second then gets no spring at all.
#
# WHAT THIS DOES NOT FIX. A .grp rule is keyed on (residue, atom) only, so
# the atoms a terminal residue SHARES with the interior one keep the interior
# charge -- AMBER retypes those too (NALA's N is N3 at -0.3821 e, not N at
# -0.4157). Net charge against AMBER's own template, in e:
#     N-terminus   -1.2719 before, -0.59 to -0.78 after (-0.3760 for proline)
#     C-terminus   +1.0000 before, +0.18 to +0.23 after
# Getting that to zero needs the format to carry the chain position, as
# .bi.ff now does with :NTER/:CTER -- but here the position would have to
# change the TYPE of every shared atom, so it is a far larger change.
#
# The first ammonium hydrogen is also spelled H by OpenMM, and both all-atom
# protein inputs here are OpenMM-written. That atom already resolves, to the
# INTERIOR amide type: 0.0722 e too positive. A second rule for the same
# (residue, atom) cannot say otherwise.
#
AH1 ALA H1
AH2 ALA H2
AH3 ALA H3
AOXT ALA OXT
